`harness.go` (fault-injection harness with an independent oracle): the `Harness` type wraps a `Cluster` and counts every accepted admission in a separate lock-protected map; `Check()` fails the instant any `(epoch,id)` is seen twice. It provides `ScenarioCold`, `ScenarioFenced`, `ScenarioPartition` (3/2 split with concurrent duplicate replay, then heal), `ScenarioExhausted` (full window, rotation reclaims), and `RunScenarios`.

The test file adds `TestExhaustiveInterleavings`, a bounded model check that enumerates all 211 reachable-set pairs for a 5-node cluster and every shuffle of two concurrent claims' per-node steps, asserting `max admissions == 1`.

### Wiring into a real QUIC server
```go
cfg := &tls.Config{Certificates: certs, MinVersion: tls.VersionTLS13}
cluster := zerortt.NewCluster(5, 1<<16)
gate := zerortt.NewGate(cfg, cluster, nodeID)

q := tls.QUICServer(&tls.QUICConfig{TLSConfig: cfg})
for {
    ev := q.NextEvent()
    if ev.Kind == tls.QUICResumeSession {
        _ = gate.OnQUICResumeSession(&ev) // declines 0-RTT on any admission failure
    }
    // ... feed ev back to the QUIC stack ...
}
_ = q.SendSessionTicket(tls.QUICSessionTicketOptions{EarlyData: true})
```

