`go test -race -count=10 ./...` also passes. Key checks:

| Check | State driven | Property proved |
|---|---|---|
| `cold` | healthy | first admission wins, immediate replay rejected |
| `fenced` | epoch rotated, lagging node | pre-boundary tickets rejected, fresh ones single-use |
| `partition` | 3/2 split, concurrent duplicate | only majority admits; minority fails closed; post-heal replay rejected |
| `exhausted` | full window | overflow fails closed; rotation reclaims and fences |
| `TestExhaustiveInterleavings` | model check, 211 set pairs | every shuffle of two claims ⇒ ≤1 admission |
| `TestRandomizedInterleavings` | 24 goroutines + churn + rotations, `-race` | independent oracle sees no double admission |
| `TestTLSResumptionSingleUse` | real `crypto/tls` | first resumption admitted, exact replay declined, fallback full handshake |

**Limitations:** during a partition the minority cannot admit 0-RTT (deliberate; CAP forbids both strict single-use and availability). Go emits `QUICResumeSession` before binder verification, so a forged ClientHello can burn a ticket (availability DoS, not a safety break). For production, make `nodeStore` durable (WAL/replicated log + fsync before ack). No wall-clock is used, so clock skew cannot widen the window.
